1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
People who constantly set sky-high expectations for themselves often crumble when faced with obstacles.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,000
It's not because they lack intelligence or ambition.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,000
It's because they haven't built up the emotional endurance to navigate failure.

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:18,000
They haven't developed the inner strength to withstand adversity.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:26,000
And yet, that resilience, that quiet, persistent strength, is what matters most when it comes to real success.

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Not talent, not grades, not test scores.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Just the ability to keep going when everything is falling apart.

8
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:38,000
The truth is, I don't know how to teach resilience in a classroom.

9
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:41,000
I can't hand it to you through a lecture or a textbook.

10
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:52,000
The only way people seem to gain it is through pain, through real suffering, through life punching them in the gut, again and again, until they learn how to stand back up.

11
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Greatness doesn't come from raw intelligence.

12
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,000
It doesn't emerge from having the highest IQ or winning every academic competition.

13
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,000
It comes from character, and character is not something you're born with.

14
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:07,000
It's something that's shaped over time.

15
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:09,000
And the chisel that shapes it?

16
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,000
It's suffering.

17
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:13,000
It's the heartbreak of failure.

18
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:17,000
It's the disappointment of being told, you're not enough.

19
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:21,000
It's the loneliness of trying your best and still falling short.

20
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:27,000
It's the deep questioning of your own worth and the strength to keep moving forward anyway.

21
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:37,000
So, to all of you brilliant Stanford students, to those of you who've spent your lives excelling, achieving and aiming high, I say this with sincerity.

22
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,000
I hope you suffer.

23
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:44,000
Not because I want you to fail, but because I want you to become unbreakable.

24
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:49,000
I want you to know the kind of pain that forges depth, grit and authenticity.

25
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:52,000
I want you to learn what you're truly made of.

26
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,000
And you can't learn that from success alone.

